    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> believed their religion had been revealed to them by seers, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tages">Tages</a> and Vegoia, and consulted the gods through signs, divination by animal entrails, thunder and lightning.</li><li>No Etruscan sacred text survives except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liber-linteus">Liber Linteus</a>; the Etrusca Disciplina, a corpus of sacred books, is known only through Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> and Valerius Maximus.</li><li>Roman haruspices and noble families claiming Etruscan descent carried the religion into Roman public life long after the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> had forgotten the language.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Piacenza liver, a bronze model of a sheep’s liver from the 1st or 2nd century CE, is divided into 40 cells that carry the names of gods.</p></aside>
